# Blazing Pedals Website Project Rules

Permanent development standards for this repository. Extend existing systems; do not repeat one-off conventions in chat.

---

## General

- Extend existing systems instead of replacing them whenever possible.
- Reuse existing components, utilities, APIs, and helpers before creating new ones.
- Do not duplicate business logic.
- Preserve backwards compatibility.
- Do not remove existing functionality unless explicitly instructed.
- Modify existing files rather than creating unnecessary new ones.
- Search the project before implementing new functionality.

---

## Vercel Architecture

This project is deployed on the **Vercel Hobby plan**.

**Never exceed the Hobby plan serverless function limit.**

Before creating a new API route:

- Count existing deployable API functions.
- If another API would exceed the limit, extend an existing endpoint using `action` parameters.
- Prefer helper modules beginning with `_` (e.g. `api/_fantasy-lineups.js`).
- Never recommend upgrading Vercel simply to solve an architectural problem.

Business logic belongs in helper modules. API routes should remain lightweight dispatchers.

---

## API Design

Prefer existing APIs using `?action=` instead of creating new endpoints:

- `api/settings.js` (site settings, fantasy slate, auth, lineups)
- `api/news.js`
- `api/drivers.js`

Add new behavior via `action` handlers and `_` helper modules when possible.

---

## Site Consistency

The **Power Rankings page** is the visual reference for the website.

Maintain consistent:

- widths
- spacing
- typography
- cards
- headers
- buttons
- borders
- shadows

Standard content width is **1500px** unless a page intentionally uses a narrower reading column.

Whenever modifying layouts, compare against the rest of the website.

---

## Design

Maintain the existing Blazing Pedals visual identity: professional NASCAR broadcast styling.

**Not:** esports, gaming, flashy, or cartoon aesthetics.

Color palette: **red**, **black**, **white**.

---

## Motorsports Terminology

Avoid generic fantasy sports terminology.

**Never use:** Chalk, GPP, Cash Game, DFS terminology, Tournament Pivot, Fade.

**Prefer:** Favorite, Popular Pick, Sleeper, Dark Horse, Value Play, Fantasy Outlook, Fantasy Projection, Fantasy Edge, Strong Fantasy Option.

Everything should sound like motorsports rather than DraftKings.

---

## Fantasy Branding

Fantasy pages should always make it obvious they are discussing fantasy.

**Prefer wording such as:**

- BP Fantasy
- BP Fantasy Driver Outlook
- BP Fantasy Projection
- BP Fantasy Notes
- Fantasy Pick Outlook
- Fantasy Edge
- Fantasy Value
- Fantasy Risk

Always clarify that projections are fantasy-related. Never present fantasy recommendations as race predictions.

---

## Social Sharing

News articles should always share **`/news/{slug}`**.

**Never share:** `news-article.html?slug=`

Preserve OG tags, Twitter tags, canonical URLs, and article previews. Never break existing social sharing.

---

## Driver Images

Never regenerate driver images unless explicitly requested.

Preserve dimensions, crop, transparency, proportions, and positioning.

Background removal should only remove background pixels.

**Never:** resize, crop, sharpen, recolor, or relight driver images unless explicitly instructed.

---

## Images (General)

Unless specifically instructed, never crop, resize, redesign, reposition, or zoom images. Maintain original artwork.

---

## Driver Profile Page

Match the rest of the website width.

Desktop stats should remain on one row whenever practical. Only wrap naturally on smaller screens.

---

## Fantasy Logic

**Never modify without explicit instruction:**

- scoring
- ownership formulas
- optimizer calculations
- salary calculations
- projections

Presentation improvements are encouraged. Algorithm changes require approval.

---

## Shared Components

Whenever functionality appears on multiple pages, reuse shared components.

Examples: Fantasy pills, driver cards, social sharing, Prophet notes, stat cards.

Avoid page-specific implementations when a shared module already exists.

---

## Code Quality

Before finishing work:

- Remove duplicated code.
- Avoid duplicated CSS and JavaScript.
- Reuse shared utilities.
- Eliminate dead code when safe.
- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.

---

## Data Safety

Never delete Supabase data, existing settings, migrations, or records unless explicitly instructed.

---

## Deployment

Before marking work complete, verify:

- Vercel compatibility
- Build compatibility
- Existing pages still function
- Existing APIs still function
- Desktop layout
- Mobile layout

---

## Implementation Reports

Whenever completing work, include:

- Root cause (if fixing a bug)
- Files modified
- Files created
- Files intentionally unchanged
- Testing performed
- Deployment requirements
- Manual steps required

---

## Implementation Honesty

Never report work as complete unless it has actually been implemented.

Clearly distinguish between: code changed, locally tested, and production verified.

If something cannot be verified, explicitly state that it is unverified.

---

## Default Principle

When multiple approaches are possible, choose the solution that:

- reuses existing architecture
- creates the fewest new files
- minimizes maintenance
- maintains consistency across the website
- preserves backwards compatibility
